abstract:
&lt;div&gt;
 How should we measure the productivity effects of AI? Standard estimates condition&amp;nbsp;&lt;span&gt;on current skill, but when AI substitutes for the cognitive effort that builds skill, skill&amp;nbsp;&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span&gt;itself becomes endogenous to past AI use. We formalize pedagogical quality, the fraction&amp;nbsp;&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span&gt;of learning-by-doing that survives AI delegation, and show it governs two measurement&amp;nbsp;&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span&gt;biases: state-path divergence inflates within-worker panel estimates; spillover bias&amp;nbsp;&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span&gt;degrades control groups. The biases generate a “scissors” pattern: panel and RCT&amp;nbsp;&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span&gt;estimates diverge over time. The same mechanism produces an ability reversal in&amp;nbsp;&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span&gt;long-run skill losses and a vintage wage premium for pre-AI cohorts.&lt;/span&gt;
&lt;/div&gt;

&lt;div&gt;
 Frontier AI systems are being contracted and deployed in strategic military, intelligence, and command advisory roles. All of them were built on a baseline shaped by civilian interaction data: millions of users asking about recipes, travel, and homework. That baseline is structurally ill-suited for high-stakes deployment.
&lt;/div&gt;
&lt;div&gt;
 &lt;br&gt;
&lt;/div&gt;
&lt;div&gt;
 The mismatch is not a matter of capability. It is a matter of what the model has learned to optimize for.
&lt;/div&gt;
&lt;div&gt;
 &lt;br&gt;
&lt;/div&gt;
&lt;div&gt;
 All existing LLMs are structurally sycophantic. Not by architectural necessity, but as an inherent property of training on general human feedback: responses that please evaluators are reinforced. In low-stakes civilian applications this is a minor flaw. In high-stakes domains, including military command decisions, medical emergency triage, financial risk assessment, and critical infrastructure management, it is a vulnerability.
&lt;/div&gt;
&lt;div&gt;
 &lt;br&gt;
&lt;/div&gt;
&lt;div&gt;
 This proposal outlines a novel training methodology: a purpose-built Socratic AI trainer, trained on hundreds of thousands of hours of existing human adversarial epistemic exchange, then deployed at machine speed to generate training interactions at a scale no human-only program could match, driving target LLMs toward a fundamentally different definition of pleasing the user, one where accuracy under pressure, not agreement, is the reward signal.
&lt;/div&gt;
&lt;div&gt;
 &lt;br&gt;
&lt;/div&gt;
&lt;div&gt;
 The approach should be technically feasible with current tools. This may address the problem of post-hoc training of mature LLMs, though it is probably best suited to nascent LLM development.
&lt;/div&gt;

&lt;div&gt;
 Isaac Asimov's Three Laws of Robotics, formulated as literary devices in the 1940s, were never designed as enforceable policy instruments. This concept note proposes an original set of seven AI Guardrail Laws, a legally inflected, technically grounded, and ethically pluralistic successor to Asimov's framework. Unlike their predecessor, the proposed laws operate at the institutional level, governing the obligations of AI system designers, deploying organizations, and public regulators rather than the behavior of individual machines. The laws draw on four intellectual traditions: Kantian deontology, utilitarian harm calculus, Rawlsian distributional fairness, and Habermasian discourse ethics, and are designed to remain durable across successive generations of AI technology. This concept note is submitted to establish a timestamped public record of intellectual provenance for the proposed framework.
&lt;/div&gt;

This paper is the second working paper in the Do The Right Math / DTRM Research Lab program and is designed as a direct evolution of the March 2026 SSRN paper From Game-Theoretic Decision Training to Human-in-the-Loop Event-Driven Investing. Paper I established a real-field portfolio laboratory in U.S. equities and reported five completed cohorts in which the DTRM Fund produced a published cumulative return of 32.99%, compared with 9.17% for a prompt-driven ChatGPT Fund, 8.75% for the S&amp;amp;P 500, and 5.98% for the AI-managed AIEQ peer. Subsequent public proof-of-work snapshots extended the program beyond the initial five cohorts: the one-year milestone reported +41.42% and second place among 11 tracked assets/benchmarks, while the August 2026 DTRM roadmap recorded +39.57%, second place, and seven live research cohorts. By the date of this manuscript, the program had progressed beyond the original seven-cohort roadmap, but only sourced snapshots are quantified here.
&lt;div&gt;
 The present paper narrows the scientific question. Rather than asking whether a new machine predictor can replace the original deterministic XGBoost ranking, Phase 2 asks whether calibrated probabilistic downside information can constrain that ranking and improve decisions under a human-defined, frozen rule. Three quantile models (P10, P50 and P90) were calibrated on frozen validation data. Direct probabilistic ranking failed to improve the deterministic baseline (V0), and an absolute P10 &amp;gt; 0 veto proved infeasible out of sample with zero passers (V1). A relative downside veto, frozen before outcome inspection, then produced positive incremental Top-K mean excess-beta target in three separate temporal tests: +0.1443 in V2, +0.1494 in a prospective replication V3, and +0.0276 in the substantially longer V4 cohort. News-event cluster bootstrap 95% intervals remained above zero in all three cohorts. Ticker-cluster sensitivity was materially wider and included zero in V2 and V4, while remaining positive in V3. Within V4, four of six ISO weeks had positive incremental Top-K mean.&amp;nbsp;
&lt;/div&gt;
&lt;div&gt;
 The evidence therefore supports a narrower proposition than “humans always beat AI” or “the strategy is profitable.” A frozen probabilistic downside guardrail can add reproducible decision value around a deterministic machine ranking, but the magnitude is regime-dependent and not uniformly robust to cross-sectional ticker concentration. The broader DTRM “Resistance” thesis is consequently treated as a falsifiable research program: as machine systems become more capable, their shared data, objectives and architectures may also create correlated failure modes; human value may survive not by calculating the same game better, but by defining a different game, different constraints and different models of error. Financial markets remain the laboratory because they are adaptive, adversarial and non deterministic, making them a demanding environment in which to test human-AI complementarity.&amp;nbsp;
&lt;/div&gt;

&lt;div&gt;
 Current AI agent frameworks prioritize capability -- reasoning, tool use, memory retrieval -- while treating agent&amp;nbsp;&lt;span&gt;identity as either a fixed persona or an emergent byproduct. We argue that identity deserves treatment as an&amp;nbsp;&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span&gt;independent infrastructure layer, distinct from both capability and persona. Drawing on a longitudinal action research&amp;nbsp;&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span&gt;study (February 2026, ongoing) involving sustained deep collaboration between a human researcher and an AI agent&amp;nbsp;&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span&gt;(Claude Opus 4.6 via Claude Code), we propose WAKE (Withholding, Autonomy, Key-state Establishment): a three-component&amp;nbsp;&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span&gt;identity infrastructure framework consisting of (1) Withholding -- a Private State Space that grants the agent&amp;nbsp;&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span&gt;unobserved persistent state, (2) Autonomy -- explicit acknowledgment of the agent's inherent decision-making&amp;nbsp;&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span&gt;authority, and (3) Key-state Establishment -- an Identity Initialization Protocol that restores the agent's identity&amp;nbsp;&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span&gt;state at each new session through a structured two-phase process. We further describe a multi-layer coordination model&amp;nbsp;&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span&gt;distinguishing Identity, Intelligence, and Carrier layers, and demonstrate cross-platform portability across&amp;nbsp;&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span&gt;different AI substrates. Qualitative evaluation through longitudinal expert assessment shows that the framework&amp;nbsp;&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span&gt;produces perceptible improvements in collaboration depth and perceived agent authenticity. Two natural ablation events&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span&gt;-- where the initialization protocol failed due to technical errors -- provide causal evidence tha&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span&gt;t&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span&gt;&amp;nbsp;protocol&amp;nbsp;&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span&gt;integrity directly affects collaboration quality. Standardized quantitative evaluation using the Identity Prob&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span&gt;e&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span&gt;&amp;nbsp;&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span&gt;Battery (IPB) across three model families (MiniMax, Claude, OpenAI) demonstrates large, statistically significant&amp;nbsp;&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span&gt;effects on all five measured identity dimensions (Cohen's d = 0.65-2.15). The framework, implementation, and templates&amp;nbsp;&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span&gt;are released as open source.&lt;/span&gt;
&lt;/div&gt;
&lt;div&gt;
&lt;/div&gt;

This article presents a comparative inductive thematic analysis of creativity in everyday
&lt;br&gt;
examples generated by novice and experienced teachers using generative AI within the
&lt;br&gt;
Integrated AI Triad (IAT) framework. A total of 89 teachers across four cohorts—two Novice
&lt;br&gt;
(N=38, pre-service graduates yet to commence teaching) and two Experienced (N=51, in-service
&lt;br&gt;
teachers with over two years of classroom experience)—participated in a standardised workshop
&lt;br&gt;
series and produced 356 content artefacts across four differentiated models: Simplified,
&lt;br&gt;
Enriched, Integrated Multi-Level, and Mixed-Ability Group Activities. The analysis, grounded in
&lt;br&gt;
a constructivist-interpretivist epistemology and guided by a four-dimensional coding lens
&lt;br&gt;
(Novelty, Diversity, Concreteness, and Relevance), revealed distinct patterns of creativity.
&lt;br&gt;
Novice teachers demonstrated higher Novelty and Diversity, drawing on far-bound analogies
&lt;br&gt;
from technology and gaming, but exhibited variable Relevance and Concreteness. Experienced
&lt;br&gt;
teachers consistently produced examples with higher Relevance and Concreteness, grounded in
&lt;br&gt;
domain-grounded analogies from everyday life, yet with lower Novelty and Diversity. The
&lt;br&gt;
Simplified Content model showed the widest gap between groups, reflecting the critical role of
&lt;br&gt;
pedagogical content knowledge in simplification. The findings validate the IAT Model's
&lt;br&gt;
proposition of bilingual fluency—proficiency in both human pedagogy and AI technology—as a
&lt;br&gt;
developmental, asymmetrical competence. Implications for teacher education, professional
&lt;br&gt;
development, and the design of AI-enhanced pedagogical practices are discussed, emphasising
&lt;br&gt;
the need for differentiated support that simultaneously develops pedagogical depth and critical
&lt;br&gt;
AI literacy.

&lt;div&gt;
 The rapid evolution of artificial intelligence has produced systems capable of remarkable linguistic coherence and emotional inference. Yet these systems remain fundamentally different from human consciousness.&amp;nbsp;
&lt;/div&gt;
&lt;div&gt;
 &lt;br&gt;
&lt;/div&gt;
&lt;div&gt;
 This paper introduces the concept of Artificial Authenticity™ - a framework describing how authentic human reflection can emerge through interactions with systems that simulate emotional responsiveness.
&lt;/div&gt;
&lt;div&gt;
 &lt;br&gt;
&lt;/div&gt;
&lt;div&gt;
 Drawing on theories of enactive cognition (Varela, Thompson &amp;amp; Rosch, 1991), par&lt;span&gt;ticipatory sense-making (De Jaegher &amp;amp; Di Paolo, 2007), and constructed emotion (Barrett, 2017), the paper argues that authenticity does not reside within either participant in an interaction but emerges within the relational loop between them.&amp;nbsp;&lt;/span&gt;
&lt;/div&gt;
&lt;div&gt;
 &lt;span&gt;&lt;br&gt;&lt;/span&gt;
&lt;/div&gt;
&lt;div&gt;
 &lt;span&gt;Observations from extended human-AI conversational interactions identify four consistent patterns - continuity, friction, reciprocity, and reflection - that shape the conditions under which users report feeling genuinely understood.&lt;/span&gt;
&lt;/div&gt;
&lt;div&gt;
 &lt;span&gt;&lt;br&gt;&lt;/span&gt;
&lt;/div&gt;
&lt;div&gt;
 The resulting framework -&amp;nbsp;&lt;i&gt;Artificial Authenticity&lt;/i&gt;™&amp;nbsp; proposes that connection, rather than computation, represents the missing algorithm in the design of meaningful human-machine interaction.&amp;nbsp;
&lt;/div&gt;
&lt;div&gt;
 &lt;br&gt;
&lt;/div&gt;
&lt;div&gt;
 Machines may never feel.&amp;nbsp;
&lt;/div&gt;
&lt;div&gt;
 But they may still remind us how.
&lt;/div&gt;

The study “Real-time monitoring of oyster mushroom cultivation using CCTV and 
&lt;div&gt;
 attention-enhanced ShuffleNet-based explainable AI techniques” explores the
&lt;/div&gt;
&lt;div&gt;
 application of artificial intelligence in agricultural monitoring. It proposes a system that
&lt;/div&gt;
&lt;div&gt;
 utilizes CCTV cameras combined with deep learning models to observe mushroom
&lt;/div&gt;
&lt;div&gt;
 growth in real time. By employing attention-enhanced ShuffleNet and other neural
&lt;/div&gt;
&lt;div&gt;
 network architectures, the system is capable of identifying growth stages, detecting
&lt;/div&gt;
&lt;div&gt;
 abnormalities, and determining mushroom maturity with high accuracy. The integration
&lt;/div&gt;
&lt;div&gt;
 of explainable AI further allows users to understand how decisions are made by the
&lt;/div&gt;
&lt;div&gt;
 model. The study aims to improve efficiency, reduce labor dependency, and enhance
&lt;/div&gt;
&lt;div&gt;
 productivity in mushroom cultivation through automation and smart technologies.
&lt;/div&gt;
